      "title": "Moab, Tribute, and External Evidence",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_4",
          "text": "The narrative describes Mesha of Moab as a sheep-master who rendered Israel heavy annual tribute: the wool of one hundred thousand lambs and one hundred thousand rams. Upon Ahab's death, Mesha rebels against Israelite overlordship. This Moab-Israel conflict finds corroboration in the Mesha Stele, a major Iron Age West Semitic inscription providing external historical testimony to Moabite-Israelite relations during this period. The stele confirms the pattern of tribute and vassalage typical of ancient Near Eastern political relationships.",
